;; C19 (arc 278) — POSITIVE CONTROL for the NESTED type renderer, `check::format_type_inner`.
;;
;; Driven by `diagnostic_output_is_deterministic.rs`'s
;; `the_nested_type_renderer_is_driven_and_renders_no_counter`. It exists because a measurement
;; said this arm needed one: the C19 render fix touched TWO renderers in `check.rs`, and a sweep of
;; all 280 `.wat.bad` with the inner arm marked showed **zero** of them reached it. So "break one
;; site, the gate still reds" — the proof that BOTH renderers are covered — was unprovable for the
;; inner one, and a mutation there would have shipped silently green.
;;
;; WHAT MAKES IT REACH THAT ARM: a tuple. `format_type`'s Parametric arm recurses through
;; `format_type` (each binder element is a standalone keyword), so a parametric type's arguments
;; never reach `format_type_inner`. Its Tuple arm does — tuple ELEMENTS are colon-stripped. So the
;; body's type must be a TUPLE whose elements are undetermined:
;;
;;   []                  -> (Vector :- [?])   an empty vector literal: element type unresolved
;;   (:wat::core::List)  -> (List :- [?])      a zero-arg List call: element type unresolved
;;
;; and the declared `-> :wat::core::bool` forces the whole thing into a ReturnTypeMismatch's `got`.
;; That reaches the inner arm TWICE in one message, both nested inside a Parametric (`Vector`'s
;; and `List`'s own element type).
;;
;; ⛔ MERGE NOTE (replay #352): grok's own header (and this fixture's original design) expected
;; `(:wat::core::List)` to infer as a BARE unresolved variable directly as a tuple element, not
;; wrapped in a `List` parametric — giving a THIRD reach path (a direct, non-nested tuple element)
;; alongside the nested-Parametric one. Measured on this tree: it infers as
;; `(wat::core::List :- [_])` instead — main's inference already knows the call's result is a
;; `List`; only its element type is unresolved, so the outer type is not itself a free variable.
;; The mutation-proof purpose (a regression in `format_type_inner`'s `Var` arm must redden this
;; fixture) still holds — both tuple elements reach that arm via their own nested Parametric — the
;; "direct tuple element" sub-case grok's own prose describes is simply not reachable through this
;; construction on this tree. `INNER_RENDERER_EXPECTED_GOT` in
;; `diagnostic_output_is_deterministic.rs` was updated to the real, measured value.
;;
;; ★ IT IS ALSO THE ONE MESSAGE IN THE CORPUS THAT CARRIES TWO **DISTINCT** UNKNOWNS, and it is
;; here on purpose. C19 chose to render an unresolved variable as the wildcard `_` rather than
;; renumber per diagnostic, and the price of that choice is exactly this: pre-fix these read
;; `[INNERVAR3764]` and `INNERVAR3765` — two different variables — and post-fix both read `_`, so
;; a reader cannot tell them apart from one variable used twice. The cost is not an argument in a
;; comment; it is a line of output this repo runs on every floor.
(:wat::core::defn :user::main [] -> :wat::core::bool
  (:wat::core::Tuple [] (:wat::core::List)))
